description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:86-98 declares Sha256 with this top-level docstring:

    /// SHA-256 digest carried on the JMAP wire as a 64-character
    /// lowercase hex string (draft-atwood-jmap-cid-00 §2).
    ///
    /// Construct with:
    ///
    /// - [Sha256::from_hex] — validates a candidate hex string.
    /// - [Sha256::from_bytes] — formats 32 raw digest bytes into a
    ///   canonical lowercase-hex string. Infallible.
    ///
    /// Wire format is the bare hex string — #[serde(try_from, into)] drives ...

But the type also implements (digest.rs:168-187):

    impl TryFrom<String> for Sha256
    impl TryFrom<&str> for Sha256
    impl std::str::FromStr for Sha256

…and via #[derive(Deserialize)] + #[serde(try_from = \"String\")] it can be deserialised from any serde input. The \"Construct with\" rustdoc enumerates only 2 of the 5 publicly-stable construction paths.

API-contract impact: a caller scanning rustdoc on the type to see \"how do I make one of these\" will see from_hex / from_bytes and miss that .parse::<Sha256>() or sha256: Sha256 = some_string.try_into()? also work. They will write extra glue (\"let s: String = ...; let sha = Sha256::from_hex(&s)?;\") where parse() or try_into() would be more idiomatic. Worse, in API surface review they may believe these conversions don't exist and write defensive code around the gap.

Suggested replacement:

    /// Construct with:
    ///
    /// - [Sha256::from_hex] / [TryFrom<&str>] / [TryFrom<String>] /
    ///   [FromStr::from_str] — validate a candidate hex string against
    ///   the ABNF.
    /// - [Sha256::from_bytes] — format 32 raw digest bytes into the
    ///   canonical lowercase-hex string. Infallible.
    /// - Serde deserialization — validates via TryFrom<String>; same
    ///   ABNF check as the explicit constructors.

The same docstring should also note that the four validating paths share the same validation logic — currently this is described only at the impl-block level (\"every deserialize path applies the same ABNF check from_hex does\") and not at the type level where API scanners look first.
